    "insert": "Generative AI is transforming healthcare through advancements in diagnostics, personalized medicine, \nand drug discovery. However, biases in training data pose significant challenges, particularly \nin under-representing minority populations and perpetuating disparities in healthcare outcomes. \nThis research focuses on identifying the origins of biases in data collection and annotation, \nthe role of demographic under-representation, and evaluating the effectiveness of current bias mitigation strategies, \nsuch as fairness-aware algorithms. Grounded in theories of algorithmic fairness and data governance, \nthe study employs a mixed-methods approach, including quantitative analysis of healthcare datasets, \nqualitative case studies on annotation practices, and expert interviews. By addressing systemic issues in data pipelines, \nthis work aims to develop ethical and equitable AI systems for healthcare applications, \ncontributing practical solutions informed by robust theoretical and methodological frameworks.\n\nThe expose is overall well written, uses comprehensive language or explains \nterminology where necessary. The focus is clearly set on data collection and \ncuration, where the root causes for the introduction of biases are believed to \nlie (see second paragraph on p. 1). This positions the research nicely in the gap found in current studies and \nother works, which are also cited correctly throughout the document. \nResearch questions are mostly chosen appropriately for this goal. Especially \nthe first question, however, may be too broadly phrased, which can cause issues \nwith the overall manageability of the studies, and their results, that are to be conducted over the \ncourse of the research. To achieve a more manageable problem space, the categories \nof biases could be further narrowed down, and also the relevant domains of data \nsets; in this case the medical domain. This is already done or at least hinted at \nin the other research questions or in the approach section of the document. It would \nbe beneficial to already clearly state this in the first research question, so that there \nare no misconceptions about the scope of the research -- for both the reader, and \nthe research conducting party. To meaningfully compare the sort of biases discussed \nin the document from the medical sector with other domains, the planned research \nhas to be carried out first, so that it can then be put in contrast to biases in \nmore generalized contexts. Especially considering that investigating intersections \nof multiple biases, as proposed in the second paragraph of the approach section, \nis already quite an extensive task. Variable isolation is already quite difficult \nwith this goal in mind, which has to be taken into account, to prevent introducing \nbiases into the proposed research. Concrete steps on how to avoid introducing these own \nbiases are missing from \"4. Addressing Challenges\" in the approach section.\nWith all this in mind, the title should also be more tangible; specifically mentioning \ndata collection and curation makes sense, because that is what is referenced in \nmore detail later on in the document.\n\nOverall, the research proposal is mostly well-structured. The schedule may be too tight, \ndepending on whether the research questions are narrowed down as described above.\n\n"
